## What it does not check, stated so it is not over-trusted

The checker verifies countable claims against the commit history, in all
{{len .Documents}} documents that carry a state line. It does **not**:

- **See prose it cannot derive a number from.** Two rounds of this stage were
  earlier remediations editing the record's past finding and section text so the
  numbers would agree. That limit was verified by mutation, not assumed — and
  the first version of the claim was wrong: it named an experiment that was a
  no-op, because the text it proposed to mutate already read the value it
  proposed. The experiment that establishes it is committing an edit of that
  finding's text and resolution cell with a clean tree and every live count
  correct, and observing the checker pass.
- **Check prose.** The state line is the machine-checked claim; a figure
  restated in prose around it is commentary, and a wrong figure there is caught
  only by the narrow shape list below. An earlier whole-file first-match scan
  could be satisfied by a table row while the real figure was wrong, which is
  why the marked state line replaced it.
- **Read the state line's position or indentation.** Any top-level line opening
  with the marker is accepted, so a line nested under a bullet, or placed in a
  footer where no reader of that document would see it, passes. This is recorded
  rather than fixed, because tightening it risks failing an edit that changed
  nothing.
- **Survive the state line being rewrapped.** It is a single line by design,
  because a pattern spanning prose broke on rewrapping — but that relocated the
  fragility onto a line that must not be wrapped, in a repository that wraps
  everything else.
- **Reach past ninety-nine.** English has no ordinal for a round hundred, the
  helper naming the expected spelling emits digits there, and the state-line
  patterns read letters — so at a hundredth remediation the checker would report
  that the document states no such claim. It fails closed and loudly, and the
  stage will close long before then, but "no ceiling" is a claim about the
  *reading*, not about every count a record might one day carry.
- **Read only the first number in a pending row's scope cell.** The reader
  returns the first parseable token, so a scope reading "the one hundred and
  first remediation" resolves to 1. Unreachable below a hundred, fails closed,
  and stated here because a round raised its absence and the next found it
  still absent.
- **Hold a hand-maintained classification of what a remediation is.** A commit
  is a remediation when its subject carries `{{.SubjectPrefix}}` and is not one
  of the {{len .Bookkeeping}} bookkeeping subjects listed above. Both halves
  were wrong in opposite directions once: a whitelist of remediation prefixes
  under-counted the commit that introduced the checker, and counting everything
  except named subjects over-counted an unrelated workflow commit and
  invalidated the count in every document. The residual weakness is that **a new
  bookkeeping commit must be added to that list by hand** — one row, and this
  manifest states it, but it is still a hand-edit.
- **Derive the remediation sequence from anything but the history.** The
  record's own audit trail cannot be protected by this checker: a commit that
  edits a past finding to make the numbers agree edits the only copy of what a
  round found.
- **Detect a renamed section heading.** An earlier version scoped each claim to
  the section stating it and failed closed when the bound heading went missing,
  by reporting that the header stated no such claim. **That assertion was
  deleted** when the header stopped restating the ordinal, and the unused helper
  went with it, so a renamed heading is now invisible rather than fail-closed —
  a regression one remediation introduced and recorded, and the dead helper has
  since been removed here rather than left as a discarded reader.

Two shape lists in the checker are **lists of wordings, not rules**, and are
stated as such because a wording not in them evades the check. One catches prose
that understates the coverage:

- `for this one file`, `this one record`, `the other four documents`,
  `are ungated`, `is not gated`, `only thing read`

The other catches a live restatement of a figure that belongs on the state line,
{{len .LiveShapes}} shapes in total:

{{range .LiveShapes}}- `{{.}}`
{{end}}

A blacklist loses to paraphrase: one round defeated the first list's six
phrasings with a sentence matching none of them, which is a reason to distrust
any list of six rather than a reason to claim the list is gone. What closes the
class is the deletion of the restatements themselves, in the same change that
introduces them; these checks exist to catch the next author reintroducing one.

Each of these limits still needs a reviewer. A checker reduces a class of defect;
it does not replace the reading.

## Why this mechanism exists

One stage's review record needed many rounds to learn that prose discipline
cannot keep a count correct, because each remediation is itself a new commit:
correcting the count in remediation N makes it stale again in N+1. Four
consecutive rounds returned that same finding, and one escalated to REJECTED
because correcting a count had begun editing the audit trail of the rounds it
was counting.

The loop ended by fixing the checker rather than the number. Its first assertion
was that the record's count equals the history count — unachievable while
authoring, because the commit carrying the corrected count is itself the next
one. It now accepts the history count **or** one more, and only while the record
itself carries an uncommitted change. The tolerance is one, and it is conditional
on there being something to commit, which is what keeps it from becoming the
off-by-one it was written to prevent.

That sequence is worth stating plainly rather than hiding: **each fix was a new
commit, and each new commit was the thing the checker then caught.** It is the
clearest possible demonstration of the loop this mechanism exists to close, and
of why closing it needed a mechanism rather than care.

**The checker was verified by mutation, not by reading.** Setting the applied
count to a number the history does not support fails it; setting it to the number
the history does support passes. The same mutation against a past finding's text
is invisible to it, which is the limitation stated above rather than a guess.

**Defects in the checker itself, each found by the checker failing on the commit
that introduced or extended it** — which is the only evidence that a checker
like this works. It compared a cardinal against an ordinal, so a cardinal figure
was reported as needing to read as an ordinal. It assumed exactly one review per
remediation, which its own existence falsified. It classified commits by
enumerating remediation subject prefixes, so the commit that introduced it was not
itself counted. And it compared number *words*, so it could only reach twenty;
the twenty-first commit after the candidate would have broken it for good, and
archiving the record would have turned it red permanently. Each was found by
mutation or by running the checker, none by reading it.

Then the descriptions of the checker were themselves the defect. Every one of
them was hand-written prose in several documents, so each mechanism change
invalidated sentences nobody had enumerated — and nineteen consecutive review
rounds found zero product defects and nineteen bookkeeping defects, every one a
figure in one document disagreeing with a figure in another. The checkers were
collapsed into one stage-configured file and their description into this
generated file; that is the whole of the change, and it is why this file exists
instead of a paragraph in five places.